1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:02,185
December seventh, nineteen eighty nine.

2
00:00:02,205 --> 00:00:05,184
Early evening. In a prison in northern Poland,

3
00:00:05,204 --> 00:00:08,558
about fifteen hundred men were watching the evening news.

4
00:00:08,578 --> 00:00:10,807
They were waiting for one word.

5
00:00:10,827 --> 00:00:11,182
Amnesty.

6
00:00:11,902 --> 00:00:14,667
The word came. Parliament had passed an amnesty.

7
00:00:14,687 --> 00:00:16,755
But not for most of them.

8
00:00:16,775 --> 00:00:19,192
Men with repeat convictions were left out.

9
00:00:19,212 --> 00:00:21,280
Within minutes, the prison was burning.

10
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:24,380
Over the next three days,

11
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:27,621
three prisons in northern Poland fell into the hands

12
00:00:27,641 --> 00:00:29,781
of their inmates. Guards opened fire.

13
00:00:29,801 --> 00:00:31,941
Prisoners killed prisoners. By the end,

14
00:00:31,961 --> 00:00:34,102
at least six men were dead.

15
00:00:34,122 --> 00:00:36,622
Exactly how many, and who killed them,

16
00:00:36,642 --> 00:00:37,702
is still disputed.

17
00:00:38,422 --> 00:00:40,990
Forty four guards would later be charged.

18
00:00:41,010 --> 00:00:42,839
Not one was ever convicted.

19
00:00:42,859 --> 00:00:44,317
And on December ninth,

20
00:00:44,337 --> 00:00:47,645
Netflix releases a drama series inspired by these events.

21
00:00:47,665 --> 00:00:49,494
Here is what really happened.

22
00:00:51,234 --> 00:00:53,374
Chapter 1. The year of freedom.

23
00:00:55,474 --> 00:00:58,938
Nineteen eighty nine was the year Poland broke free.

24
00:00:58,958 --> 00:01:02,422
In April, the communist government and the Solidarity opposition

25
00:01:02,442 --> 00:01:04,358
signed the Round Table agreements.

26
00:01:04,378 --> 00:01:07,841
In June, Poland held its first partly free elections.

27
00:01:07,861 --> 00:01:11,325
Solidarity won almost every seat it was allowed to

28
00:01:11,345 --> 00:01:11,712
contest.

29
00:01:12,432 --> 00:01:15,191
In September, Tadeusz Mazowiecki became prime minister.

30
00:01:15,211 --> 00:01:18,763
The first non communist head of government in the

31
00:01:18,783 --> 00:01:21,144
Eastern Bloc since the nineteen forties.

32
00:01:21,864 --> 00:01:23,940
Political prisoners had already walked free.

33
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:27,084
A law passed in May wiped out convictions for

34
00:01:27,104 --> 00:01:29,529
political offenses going back to nineteen eighty.

35
00:01:29,549 --> 00:01:32,673
Men who had sat in cells for opposing the

36
00:01:32,693 --> 00:01:34,769
regime were now sitting in parliament.

37
00:01:35,489 --> 00:01:36,802
And inside Poland's prisons,

38
00:01:36,822 --> 00:01:39,800
tens of thousands of ordinary criminals watched all of

39
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:42,798
this on television. And they asked a simple question.

40
00:01:42,818 --> 00:01:44,797
If everything is changing out there,

41
00:01:44,817 --> 00:01:46,130
why not in here?

42
00:01:47,450 --> 00:01:50,261
Many of them had reasons to feel the old

43
00:01:50,281 --> 00:01:51,520
system had been unfair.

44
00:01:51,540 --> 00:01:52,778
In nineteen eighty five,

45
00:01:52,798 --> 00:01:55,609
the communist government had passed a law that made

46
00:01:55,629 --> 00:01:57,497
sentences for property crimes much harsher.

47
00:01:58,217 --> 00:02:01,500
A prison sociologist later described what spread through the

48
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:03,702
cells that year. An amnesty sickness.

49
00:02:03,722 --> 00:02:05,537
Everyone was waiting for freedom.

50
00:02:06,257 --> 00:02:08,843
A guard from the prison in Goleniów remembered it

51
00:02:08,863 --> 00:02:11,449
the same way. We knew they were waiting for

52
00:02:11,469 --> 00:02:13,476
amnesty, he said. If there wasn't one,

53
00:02:13,496 --> 00:02:14,924
there would be a riot.

54
00:02:15,864 --> 00:02:17,947
Chapter 2. Waiting for the word.

55
00:02:20,047 --> 00:02:22,173
The unrest began in early June,

56
00:02:22,193 --> 00:02:23,962
the month of the elections.

57
00:02:23,982 --> 00:02:26,108
In dozens of prisons and jails,

58
00:02:26,128 --> 00:02:27,539
inmates refused to work.

59
00:02:27,559 --> 00:02:28,970
Some refused to eat.

60
00:02:28,990 --> 00:02:31,473
They demanded better pay for prison labor,

61
00:02:31,493 --> 00:02:32,547
and better conditions.

62
00:02:33,267 --> 00:02:36,200
In August, a bill for an amnesty was submitted

63
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:39,154
to parliament. Now prisoners had something concrete to wait

64
00:02:39,174 --> 00:02:39,483
for.

65
00:02:40,203 --> 00:02:42,659
The signals they got were mixed.

66
00:02:42,679 --> 00:02:46,373
In late September, a deputy justice minister said no

67
00:02:46,393 --> 00:02:48,437
amnesty was planned. Days later,

68
00:02:48,457 --> 00:02:50,501
the justice minister, Aleksander Bentkowski,

69
00:02:50,521 --> 00:02:53,390
appeared on television with members of parliament,

70
00:02:53,410 --> 00:02:54,628
appealing for calm.

71
00:02:55,348 --> 00:02:58,887
Then an opposition lawmaker announced that a solution was

72
00:02:58,907 --> 00:03:01,654
coming. One that would cover all prisoners,

73
00:03:01,674 --> 00:03:04,027
including repeat offenders. To varying degrees.

74
00:03:04,747 --> 00:03:07,184
Prisoners heard what they wanted to hear.

75
00:03:07,204 --> 00:03:07,886
All prisoners.

76
00:03:09,206 --> 00:03:10,534
Some did not wait.

77
00:03:10,554 --> 00:03:11,883
In Olsztyn, in September,

78
00:03:11,903 --> 00:03:13,231
prisoners overpowered an officer,

79
00:03:13,251 --> 00:03:16,266
and about fifty of them climbed onto the roof.

80
00:03:16,286 --> 00:03:18,625
A member of parliament talked them down,

81
00:03:18,645 --> 00:03:20,311
without the use of force.

82
00:03:21,031 --> 00:03:23,963
In Gdańsk, according to one Polish history account,

83
00:03:23,983 --> 00:03:27,284
more than seven hundred prisoners left their cells in

84
00:03:27,304 --> 00:03:30,237
November, and occupied the roofs for two weeks.

85
00:03:30,257 --> 00:03:32,820
That protest also ended with an agreement.

86
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:36,127
So far, the message seemed to be that protest

87
00:03:36,147 --> 00:03:38,734
worked. And that the new Poland was willing to

88
00:03:38,754 --> 00:03:39,024
listen.

89
00:03:39,744 --> 00:03:42,762
On November sixteenth, the lower house of parliament,

90
00:03:42,782 --> 00:03:45,800
the Sejm, passed its version of the amnesty.

91
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:47,699
Repeat offenders were left out.

92
00:03:47,719 --> 00:03:51,117
The Senate, the new upper house dominated by Solidarity,

93
00:03:51,137 --> 00:03:54,534
pushed back. It proposed cutting their sentences by a

94
00:03:54,554 --> 00:03:54,914
quarter.

95
00:03:55,634 --> 00:03:58,597
The final decision would come back to the Sejm.

96
00:03:58,617 --> 00:03:59,923
On Thursday, December seventh.

97
00:04:00,863 --> 00:04:02,969
Chapter 3. The evening news.

98
00:04:05,069 --> 00:04:08,399
On December seventh, the Sejm rejected the Senate's proposal.

99
00:04:08,419 --> 00:04:09,887
According to one account,

100
00:04:09,907 --> 00:04:11,376
the vote was overwhelming.

101
00:04:11,396 --> 00:04:12,865
The amnesty became law.

102
00:04:13,585 --> 00:04:15,278
On paper, it was generous.

103
00:04:15,298 --> 00:04:18,362
Short sentences of up to two years were suspended.

104
00:04:18,382 --> 00:04:21,446
Sentences of up to ten years were cut in

105
00:04:21,466 --> 00:04:24,187
half. Longer ones were cut by a third.

106
00:04:24,207 --> 00:04:26,243
Twenty five year sentences became fifteen.

107
00:04:26,263 --> 00:04:27,956
Even death sentences were commuted,

108
00:04:27,976 --> 00:04:29,327
to twenty five years.

109
00:04:30,047 --> 00:04:32,517
But the exclusions were long.

110
00:04:32,537 --> 00:04:35,506
Murder. Robbery. Burglary. Especially brutal rape.

111
00:04:35,526 --> 00:04:37,499
Abuse of family members.

112
00:04:38,219 --> 00:04:39,709
And one more group.

113
00:04:39,729 --> 00:04:43,109
Multiple repeat offenders, as defined by article sixty of

114
00:04:43,129 --> 00:04:44,998
the criminal code. For them,

115
00:04:45,018 --> 00:04:46,508
the amnesty gave nothing.

116
00:04:46,528 --> 00:04:47,642
Not a day.

117
00:04:49,162 --> 00:04:51,802
One example from Polish historians makes it concrete.

118
00:04:51,822 --> 00:04:53,464
A man serving eight years,

119
00:04:53,484 --> 00:04:55,127
classed as a repeat offender.

120
00:04:55,147 --> 00:04:58,120
The Senate had wanted to cut two years off

121
00:04:58,140 --> 00:05:00,115
his sentence. Under the final law,

122
00:05:00,135 --> 00:05:01,777
he would serve all eight.

123
00:05:02,497 --> 00:05:05,624
The justice ministry expected around seventeen thousand people to

124
00:05:05,644 --> 00:05:07,372
benefit. But in some prisons,

125
00:05:07,392 --> 00:05:10,519
most of the inmates were exactly the men the

126
00:05:10,539 --> 00:05:11,918
law had left out.

127
00:05:12,638 --> 00:05:15,660
And they found out the same way everyone else

128
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:17,350
did. From the evening news.

129
00:05:17,370 --> 00:05:19,041
A short bulletin called Teleexpress.

130
00:05:20,581 --> 00:05:22,396
Chapter 4. Czarne.

131
00:05:24,496 --> 00:05:27,143
Czarne is a small town in Pomerania,

132
00:05:27,163 --> 00:05:30,572
in northern Poland. Its prison held about fifteen hundred

133
00:05:30,592 --> 00:05:33,239
men. Many of them were repeat offenders,

134
00:05:33,259 --> 00:05:34,382
serving long sentences.

135
00:05:35,102 --> 00:05:37,829
Around six in the evening on December seventh,

136
00:05:37,849 --> 00:05:39,202
right after the news,

137
00:05:39,222 --> 00:05:41,949
the prisoners took the buildings inside the walls.

138
00:05:41,969 --> 00:05:43,322
The officers pulled back,

139
00:05:43,342 --> 00:05:45,382
beyond the area the prisoners controlled.

140
00:05:46,102 --> 00:05:47,439
According to one account,

141
00:05:47,459 --> 00:05:48,797
the informal prisoner leaders,

142
00:05:48,817 --> 00:05:51,851
known as senators, gathered men on the prison's central

143
00:05:51,871 --> 00:05:54,905
avenue. Their plan was to break into the armory,

144
00:05:54,925 --> 00:05:56,602
and then storm the gate.

145
00:05:57,322 --> 00:05:59,665
The pavilions were set on fire.

146
00:05:59,685 --> 00:06:01,634
Common rooms. The medical stores.

147
00:06:01,654 --> 00:06:04,784
The food stores. Flames lit up the night.

148
00:06:05,504 --> 00:06:07,885
Then the prisoners went for the way out.

149
00:06:07,905 --> 00:06:09,986
They tried to force the main gate,

150
00:06:10,006 --> 00:06:11,186
and the outer wall.

151
00:06:11,206 --> 00:06:13,287
Guards in the watch posts opened fire.

152
00:06:13,307 --> 00:06:14,788
The attack was pushed back.

153
00:06:15,508 --> 00:06:17,482
One account claims that at some point,

154
00:06:17,502 --> 00:06:20,046
prisoners had their hands on a pistol and a

155
00:06:20,066 --> 00:06:22,325
grenade launcher. Other accounts do not mention this,

156
00:06:22,345 --> 00:06:24,034
and we could not confirm it.

157
00:06:24,754 --> 00:06:27,013
Inside, the violence turned inward.

158
00:06:27,033 --> 00:06:28,837
Polish historians describe lynchings.

159
00:06:28,857 --> 00:06:30,660
Prisoners attacking other prisoners,

160
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:32,940
with knives and blunt weapons.

161
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:35,675
Old scores, settled in the dark.

162
00:06:36,395 --> 00:06:38,920
At one point, prisoners carried two dead men and

163
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:40,334
six wounded to the gate,

164
00:06:40,354 --> 00:06:41,466
and handed them over.

165
00:06:41,486 --> 00:06:44,011
One of the wounded died on the way to

166
00:06:44,031 --> 00:06:44,294
hospital.

167
00:06:45,014 --> 00:06:47,839
Prosecutors and a judge appealed to the prisoners to

168
00:06:47,859 --> 00:06:49,104
stop. The appeals failed.

169
00:06:49,124 --> 00:06:51,001
Czarne was no longer a prison.

170
00:06:51,021 --> 00:06:52,265
It was a fortress,

171
00:06:52,285 --> 00:06:55,110
held by the men it was built to hold.

172
00:06:56,850 --> 00:06:59,908
Chapter 5. Goleniów and Nowogard.

173
00:07:02,008 --> 00:07:03,501
Czarne was not alone.

174
00:07:03,521 --> 00:07:06,147
About two hundred kilometers to the west,

175
00:07:06,167 --> 00:07:09,550
near Szczecin, two more prisons full of repeat offenders

176
00:07:09,570 --> 00:07:11,440
were watching the same news.

177
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:15,460
In Goleniów, the prison administration had seen it coming.

178
00:07:15,480 --> 00:07:18,411
Reinforcements had been brought in the day before.

179
00:07:18,431 --> 00:07:21,731
On December seventh, the morning shift was kept on

180
00:07:21,751 --> 00:07:23,206
duty, armed with batons,

181
00:07:23,226 --> 00:07:24,682
shields and gas masks.

182
00:07:25,402 --> 00:07:27,592
At around five thirty in the evening,

183
00:07:27,612 --> 00:07:30,435
after the news, twenty two members of a prisoner

184
00:07:30,455 --> 00:07:31,698
committee met the governor.

185
00:07:31,718 --> 00:07:32,961
They demanded the keys,

186
00:07:32,981 --> 00:07:35,803
and the withdrawal of all officers from the cell

187
00:07:35,823 --> 00:07:36,751
blocks. He refused.

188
00:07:37,471 --> 00:07:39,552
The prisoners took two pavilions anyway,

189
00:07:39,572 --> 00:07:41,304
and the keys with them.

190
00:07:41,324 --> 00:07:43,055
Cells were set on fire.

191
00:07:43,075 --> 00:07:44,806
So were both prison libraries,

192
00:07:44,826 --> 00:07:46,207
and the hospital equipment.

193
00:07:46,927 --> 00:07:48,951
At three twenty in the morning,

194
00:07:48,971 --> 00:07:50,995
officers stormed one of the pavilions.

195
00:07:51,015 --> 00:07:53,719
By six, they had retaken the lower floors,

196
00:07:53,739 --> 00:07:56,785
and fifty four prisoners took down a barricade and

197
00:07:56,805 --> 00:07:57,126
surrendered.

198
00:07:57,846 --> 00:08:00,400
In Nowogard, about twenty five kilometers away,

199
00:08:00,420 --> 00:08:03,342
about eight hundred and forty men were held.

200
00:08:03,362 --> 00:08:05,181
At nine in the evening,

201
00:08:05,201 --> 00:08:07,388
a prisoner committee announced a strike.

202
00:08:07,408 --> 00:08:09,594
No work. Nobody leaves the cells.

203
00:08:09,614 --> 00:08:11,801
They asked for a government commission,

204
00:08:11,821 --> 00:08:14,375
and promised the protest would stay peaceful.

205
00:08:15,095 --> 00:08:17,287
It did not. The next day,

206
00:08:17,307 --> 00:08:19,500
the radio reported deaths at Czarne.

207
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:22,449
The committee's leader threatened the governor with arson.

208
00:08:22,469 --> 00:08:23,924
Doors were broken down.

209
00:08:23,944 --> 00:08:25,030
Fires were set.

210
00:08:25,750 --> 00:08:28,364
Officers used batons, firecrackers and chemical agents,

211
00:08:28,384 --> 00:08:30,246
and were forced to retreat.

212
00:08:30,266 --> 00:08:32,127
Prisoners climbed onto the roofs.

213
00:08:32,147 --> 00:08:34,008
They burned a cell block,

214
00:08:34,028 --> 00:08:35,137
and the hospital.

215
00:08:35,857 --> 00:08:37,732
Three prisons. Thousands of men.

216
00:08:37,752 --> 00:08:39,627
And a new democratic government,

217
00:08:39,647 --> 00:08:41,144
barely three months old,

218
00:08:41,164 --> 00:08:44,176
that now had to decide what to do.

219
00:08:45,716 --> 00:08:47,613
Chapter 6. The assault.

220
00:08:49,713 --> 00:08:53,037
On December eighth, the government decided that the police,

221
00:08:53,057 --> 00:08:54,894
still called the citizens' militia,

222
00:08:54,914 --> 00:08:57,123
would back up the prison service.

223
00:08:57,843 --> 00:09:00,623
On December ninth, they went into Czarne.

224
00:09:00,643 --> 00:09:03,023
Militia units, including anti terror teams,

225
00:09:03,043 --> 00:09:06,623
and prison officers. One account describes men rappelling from

226
00:09:06,643 --> 00:09:09,023
helicopters, tear gas and stun grenades,

227
00:09:09,043 --> 00:09:12,222
and an operation that took about forty minutes.

228
00:09:12,942 --> 00:09:14,520
By the end of the day,

229
00:09:14,540 --> 00:09:16,916
the prison was back in the hands of the

230
00:09:16,936 --> 00:09:18,780
state. Or what was left of it.

231
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:21,176
Most of the pavilions were burned out or wrecked.

232
00:09:21,196 --> 00:09:23,306
The prison would have to be practically rebuilt.

233
00:09:24,026 --> 00:09:27,618
Twenty six injured prisoners were taken to the prison

234
00:09:27,638 --> 00:09:30,829
hospital in Bydgoszcz. Two were in critical condition.

235
00:09:30,849 --> 00:09:32,434
Twelve had gunshot wounds.

236
00:09:32,454 --> 00:09:34,842
Thirty two prison officers needed treatment.

237
00:09:35,562 --> 00:09:38,942
In Goleniów, a senior militia officer arrived during the

238
00:09:38,962 --> 00:09:41,208
night. At one in the morning,

239
00:09:41,228 --> 00:09:44,230
the governor called on the prisoners to surrender,

240
00:09:44,250 --> 00:09:46,497
through a megaphone. One pavilion agreed.

241
00:09:46,517 --> 00:09:48,385
Then, after negotiations, the other.

242
00:09:48,405 --> 00:09:50,652
By morning, the transports had begun.

243
00:09:51,372 --> 00:09:53,273
Nowogard held out the longest.

244
00:09:53,293 --> 00:09:55,579
On the night of December ninth,

245
00:09:55,599 --> 00:09:57,116
hundreds of militia officers,

246
00:09:57,136 --> 00:09:58,654
including special units, arrived.

247
00:09:58,674 --> 00:10:02,112
At dawn, around three hundred and fifty prisoners were

248
00:10:02,132 --> 00:10:03,650
on a single roof.

249
00:10:04,370 --> 00:10:06,784
This time, the government sent negotiators.

250
00:10:06,804 --> 00:10:08,813
Among them was Władysław Frasyniuk,

251
00:10:08,833 --> 00:10:12,464
a Solidarity leader who had himself spent years in

252
00:10:12,484 --> 00:10:15,304
communist prisons. Every day in a cell,

253
00:10:15,324 --> 00:10:16,927
he would later say,

254
00:10:16,947 --> 00:10:17,739
was horror.

255
00:10:18,459 --> 00:10:21,880
At around one in the afternoon on December tenth,

256
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:23,409
the last resistance ended.

257
00:10:23,429 --> 00:10:24,938
According to Polish historians,

258
00:10:24,958 --> 00:10:28,379
nobody on either side was hurt during the surrender.

259
00:10:28,399 --> 00:10:30,673
That afternoon, the justice minister arrived,

260
00:10:30,693 --> 00:10:33,732
and promised that those responsible would face charges.

261
00:10:34,672 --> 00:10:36,139
Chapter 7. The dead.

262
00:10:38,239 --> 00:10:39,566
So how many people died?

263
00:10:40,286 --> 00:10:42,868
The honest answer is that sources disagree.

264
00:10:42,888 --> 00:10:46,212
At Czarne, the first reports spoke of three dead.

265
00:10:46,232 --> 00:10:49,557
Then five. A bulletin from the Polish press agency

266
00:10:49,577 --> 00:10:51,416
on December eighteenth said six.

267
00:10:52,136 --> 00:10:54,015
At Goleniów, one more prisoner died.

268
00:10:54,035 --> 00:10:56,232
He had been beaten by other inmates,

269
00:10:56,252 --> 00:10:58,765
and was later found burned in his cell.

270
00:10:58,785 --> 00:11:01,614
A post mortem showed he was already dead when

271
00:11:01,634 --> 00:11:02,881
the fire reached him.

272
00:11:03,601 --> 00:11:06,634
That makes at least seven across the three prisons.

273
00:11:06,654 --> 00:11:07,991
Some studies give eight.

274
00:11:08,011 --> 00:11:11,045
One academic study of the riots in the region

275
00:11:11,065 --> 00:11:12,402
mentions only one death.

276
00:11:12,422 --> 00:11:14,777
The numbers have never been fully settled.

277
00:11:15,497 --> 00:11:17,241
And then there is the bigger question.

278
00:11:17,261 --> 00:11:17,998
Who killed them?

279
00:11:19,318 --> 00:11:22,494
Some accounts describe the deaths at Czarne mainly as

280
00:11:22,514 --> 00:11:23,914
killings by other prisoners.

281
00:11:23,934 --> 00:11:25,690
But according to Polish historians,

282
00:11:25,710 --> 00:11:28,886
the local prosecutor's early findings attributed four deaths to

283
00:11:28,906 --> 00:11:31,017
prisoners, and two to guards' firearms.

284
00:11:31,037 --> 00:11:34,213
And Poland's ombudsman later concluded that most of the

285
00:11:34,233 --> 00:11:36,344
dead had been killed by bullets.

286
00:11:37,064 --> 00:11:39,326
Prisoners killing each other in the chaos.

287
00:11:39,346 --> 00:11:41,609
Or guards shooting to hold the gate.

288
00:11:41,629 --> 00:11:43,239
The truth may be both.

289
00:11:43,259 --> 00:11:46,173
But the official record never gave a final answer.

290
00:11:47,913 --> 00:11:49,926
Chapter 8. After the smoke.

291
00:11:52,026 --> 00:11:53,297
When it was over,

292
00:11:53,317 --> 00:11:54,910
the three prisons were emptied.

293
00:11:54,930 --> 00:11:56,201
According to one account,

294
00:11:56,221 --> 00:11:59,105
more than two thousand prisoners were transferred to two

295
00:11:59,125 --> 00:12:01,040
dozen other prisons around the country.

296
00:12:01,060 --> 00:12:02,976
A few hundred stayed in Czarne,

297
00:12:02,996 --> 00:12:03,944
to clean up.

298
00:12:04,664 --> 00:12:05,991
And for many of them,

299
00:12:06,011 --> 00:12:07,338
the worst part came next.

300
00:12:08,658 --> 00:12:12,419
In mid December, Polish newspapers reported that transferred prisoners

301
00:12:12,439 --> 00:12:14,939
were being beaten. Poland's first ombudsman,

302
00:12:14,959 --> 00:12:18,300
Ewa Łętowska, sent her staff to five prisons.

303
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:21,241
They interviewed one hundred and three prisoners,

304
00:12:21,261 --> 00:12:25,022
and confirmed beatings, during the transports and after arrival.

305
00:12:25,742 --> 00:12:28,635
Prosecutors investigated. In March nineteen ninety three,

306
00:12:28,655 --> 00:12:31,133
forty four prison officers were indicted,

307
00:12:31,153 --> 00:12:33,630
at a district court near Czarne.

308
00:12:34,350 --> 00:12:36,554
The case dragged on for years.

309
00:12:36,574 --> 00:12:38,036
In nineteen ninety seven,

310
00:12:38,056 --> 00:12:41,372
charges against twenty of the accused were conditionally discontinued.

311
00:12:41,392 --> 00:12:44,707
The court listed more than two thousand five hundred

312
00:12:44,727 --> 00:12:46,931
witnesses. It heard about eight hundred.

313
00:12:47,651 --> 00:12:50,083
At the end of two thousand four,

314
00:12:50,103 --> 00:12:52,185
the statute of limitations ran out.

315
00:12:52,205 --> 00:12:53,937
Fifteen years after the riot,

316
00:12:53,957 --> 00:12:57,090
the case against the remaining fifteen officers ended without

317
00:12:57,110 --> 00:12:57,791
a verdict.

318
00:12:58,511 --> 00:13:00,685
None of the officers was ever convicted.

319
00:13:02,205 --> 00:13:03,622
And the amnesty itself?

320
00:13:03,642 --> 00:13:05,058
It stood as written.

321
00:13:05,078 --> 00:13:08,290
One of the men it did help was Mariusz

322
00:13:08,310 --> 00:13:10,086
Trynkiewicz, a convicted serial killer.

323
00:13:10,106 --> 00:13:13,318
Under the amnesty, Poland's Supreme Court commuted his death

324
00:13:13,338 --> 00:13:15,114
sentence to twenty five years.

325
00:13:15,134 --> 00:13:17,987
When he was released in two thousand fourteen,

326
00:13:18,007 --> 00:13:21,219
he was immediately placed in a closed secure center.

327
00:13:21,939 --> 00:13:24,329
Thousands of first time offenders went home.

328
00:13:24,349 --> 00:13:27,083
The repeat offenders who burned their prisons stayed.

329
00:13:27,103 --> 00:13:30,181
And crimes committed during the riots were not covered

330
00:13:30,201 --> 00:13:31,558
by the amnesty either.

331
00:13:32,498 --> 00:13:34,511
Chapter 9. Who was to blame?

332
00:13:36,611 --> 00:13:39,860
Years later, the justice minister at the time,

333
00:13:39,880 --> 00:13:41,495
Aleksander Bentkowski, looked back.

334
00:13:41,515 --> 00:13:43,129
The cause, he said,

335
00:13:43,149 --> 00:13:45,172
was those unfortunate amnesty procedures.

336
00:13:45,192 --> 00:13:47,624
They were just waiting for that.

337
00:13:48,344 --> 00:13:51,368
Some on the other side believed something darker.

338
00:13:51,388 --> 00:13:54,793
A legend spread in the prisons that Solidarity itself

339
00:13:54,813 --> 00:13:56,315
was organizing the revolts.

340
00:13:56,335 --> 00:13:59,739
Jacek Kuroń, one of the movement's best known leaders,

341
00:13:59,759 --> 00:14:01,642
said it was exactly that,

342
00:14:01,662 --> 00:14:03,545
a legend. Solidarity, he said,

343
00:14:03,565 --> 00:14:04,686
had organized nothing.

344
00:14:05,406 --> 00:14:08,399
And some of the confusion came from the government

345
00:14:08,419 --> 00:14:10,407
itself. According to one Polish historian,

346
00:14:10,427 --> 00:14:13,420
a statement suggesting the amnesty would be universal was

347
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:15,093
passed down to prison directors,

348
00:14:15,113 --> 00:14:18,106
and spread in the prisons for over a month.

349
00:14:18,126 --> 00:14:20,784
The minister later said he had guaranteed nothing.

350
00:14:21,504 --> 00:14:24,293
In the Senate, one speaker put the dilemma bluntly.

351
00:14:24,313 --> 00:14:25,542
The Senate, he said,

352
00:14:25,562 --> 00:14:28,352
could not deliberate on the amnesty law with a

353
00:14:28,372 --> 00:14:29,600
pistol to its temple.

354
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:33,537
In the end, the riots did change things.

355
00:14:33,557 --> 00:14:37,177
The new government replaced prison governors and regional directors.

356
00:14:37,197 --> 00:14:40,818
And a new, Solidarity era leadership took over the

357
00:14:40,838 --> 00:14:44,458
prison service. Today, Polish media often describe Czarne as

358
00:14:44,478 --> 00:14:47,290
the biggest prison riot in postwar Europe.

359
00:14:48,230 --> 00:14:49,674
Chapter 10. Riot.

360
00:14:51,774 --> 00:14:53,120
Thirty seven years later,

361
00:14:53,140 --> 00:14:54,827
the story is coming back.

362
00:14:54,847 --> 00:14:56,876
On December ninth, Netflix releases Riot,

363
00:14:56,896 --> 00:14:59,949
a six episode Polish drama inspired by the prison

364
00:14:59,969 --> 00:15:01,657
uprisings of nineteen eighty nine.

365
00:15:01,677 --> 00:15:03,023
It is a drama,

366
00:15:03,043 --> 00:15:06,096
not a documentary. But it grows out of the

367
00:15:06,116 --> 00:15:07,803
days you have just seen.

368
00:15:08,523 --> 00:15:10,833
The real story is harder to watch.

369
00:15:10,853 --> 00:15:12,498
A country celebrating its freedom.

370
00:15:12,518 --> 00:15:15,493
Prisoners convinced that freedom was meant for them too.

371
00:15:15,513 --> 00:15:16,825
One evening news bulletin.

372
00:15:16,845 --> 00:15:18,156
And a promise that,

373
00:15:18,176 --> 00:15:19,820
depending on who you ask,

374
00:15:19,840 --> 00:15:21,152
was never really made.

375
00:15:21,872 --> 00:15:23,410
Six dead at Czarne.

376
00:15:23,430 --> 00:15:25,747
Possibly more. Forty four guards charged.

377
00:15:25,767 --> 00:15:29,252
None convicted. And a question that Poland has never

378
00:15:29,272 --> 00:15:30,031
fully answered.

379
00:15:30,751 --> 00:15:32,236
Was the riot a crime,

380
00:15:32,256 --> 00:15:34,645
or a predictable result of a broken promise?

381
00:15:34,665 --> 00:15:37,054
Tell us what you think in the comments.

382
00:15:37,074 --> 00:15:39,764
And for a riot where prisoners held an American

383
00:15:39,784 --> 00:15:40,968
prison for eleven days,

384
00:15:40,988 --> 00:15:42,473
watch our story about Lucasville,

385
00:15:42,493 --> 00:15:43,377
on this channel.
